/* book Worked example: Q: At station P (E 1000.000 m, N 2000.000 m), backsight Q has azimuth 30°00′00″. A total station reads a clockwise horizontal angle β = 75°30′00″ from PQ to R, a slope distance of 120.000 m and a zenith angle of 85°00′00″. Find the horizontal distance PR, the azimuth of PR, and the coordinates of R.
   1. Data: EP = 1000.000 m, NP = 2000.000 m, backsight azimuth = 30°00′00″, β = 75°30′00″, S = 120.000 m, z = 85°00′00″.
   2. Prism constant is zero here, so Sc = S = 120.000 m. Horizontal distance: d = Sc sin z = 120.000 × sin 85° = 120.000 × 0.996195 = 119.543 m.
   3. Azimuth of PR: A = 30°00′00″ + 75°30′00″ = 105°30′00″ = 105.5°.
   4. Easting difference: ΔE = d sin A = 119.5434 × 0.963630 = 115.196 m (keep d unrounded until the end).
   5. Northing difference: ΔN = d cos A = 119.5434 × (−0.267238) = −31.947 m.
   6. Coordinates of R: ER = 1000.000 + 115.196 = 1115.196 m; NR = 2000.000 − 31.947 = 1968.053 m.
   Answer: PR = 119.543 m, azimuth PR = A = 105°30′00″, and R = (E 1115.196 m, N 1968.053 m). */
