/* book Worked example 2: Q: From the same station P (E 1000.000 m, N 2000.000 m, RL 25.000 m), with the circle oriented on the backsight Q at azimuth 30°00′00″, the instrument height is 1.550 m. (a) The shot to R in the first example had S = 120.000 m and z = 85°00′00″ with a target height of 1.800 m: find the reduced level of R. (b) A design point D at (E 1050.000 m, N 2080.000 m) is to be staked out: find the angle to turn from the backsight and the horizontal distance to lay out.
   1. Vertical component of the shot to R: ΔV = S cos z = 120.000 × cos 85° = 120.000 × 0.087156 = 10.459 m.
   2. Reduced level of R: RLR = RLP + HI + ΔV − HT = 25.000 + 1.550 + 10.459 − 1.800 = 35.209 m.
   3. Stake-out of D: ΔE = 1050.000 − 1000.000 = +50.000 m and ΔN = 2080.000 − 2000.000 = +80.000 m, so D lies north-east of P.
   4. Distance PD = √(50.000² + 80.000²) = √(2500 + 6400) = √8900 = 94.340 m.
   5. Azimuth PD = tan⁻¹(50.000/80.000) = tan⁻¹ 0.625 = 32°00′19″ (first quadrant, both differences positive).
   6. Angle to turn clockwise from the backsight: 32°00′19″ − 30°00′00″ = 2°00′19″.
   7. In the field the display shows the remaining angle and distance as the prism is moved; the peg is driven when both read zero, then the point is measured again as a check.
   Answer: RL of R = 35.209 m. To set out D, turn 2°00′19″ clockwise from the backsight and lay out 94.340 m horizontally. */
