/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The space segment is the satellite constellation.
   - Each signal has three parts.
   - ρ is the measured pseudorange in metres; c = 299,792,458 m/s; tr is the true reception time in system (GPS) time in seconds, equal to the receiver clock reading minus δtr; ts is the satellite transmission time in system time in seconds; δtr
   - Four satellites give four pseudoranges.
   - Absolute positioning uses one receiver.
   - DGPS corrects code ranges.
   - Survey modes: static, fast static and kinematic.
   - Data acquisition in the field.
   - A receiver has an antenna, a radio-frequency front end, channels, a processor and storage.
   - GPS broadcasts its orbits in WGS84, a global geocentric datum.
*/
