/* book Worked example 2: Q: At one instant a GPS satellite is at (15 000 000, 10 000 000, 20 000 000) m in earth-centred coordinates and a receiver on the equator is at (6 378 137, 0, 0) m. Find the true range, the signal travel time, and the pseudorange the receiver records if its clock is 1.000 ms ahead. Take c = 299 792 458 m/s.
   1. Differences: Δx = 15 000 000 − 6 378 137 = 8 621 863 m, Δy = 10 000 000 m, Δz = 20 000 000 m.
   2. True range r = √(8 621 863² + 10 000 000² + 20 000 000²) = √(7.4337 × 10¹³ + 1.0 × 10¹⁴ + 4.0 × 10¹⁴) = 23 965 319 m.
   3. Travel time = r/c = 23 965 319/299 792 458 = 0.07994 s, about 80 ms.
   4. Clock bias error = c δt = 299 792 458 × 1.000 × 10⁻³ = 299 792 m. The clock is ahead, so the recorded time difference is too long.
   5. Pseudorange ρ = r + c δt = 23 965 319 + 299 792 = 24 265 111 m.
   6. Every satellite gets the same 299 792 m added, which is why δt is solved as a fourth unknown with the position.
   Answer: True range 23 965 319 m, travel time 0.0799 s, recorded pseudorange 24 265 111 m (299 792 m too long because of the 1 ms clock error). */
